{{ 'ness-instagram.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

{%- comment -%}
  Instagram strip — seven tiles, live feed.

  The photos are not settings. A Cloudflare Worker pulls the most recent
  posts on a cron, re-hosts each image to Shopify Files, and writes
  [{ src, permalink }] into the shop metafield ness.instagram_feed
  (PLAN.md, Decision #10). This section reads that metafield and renders it
  server-side, so the tiles are in the HTML on first paint — no layout shift,
  no CORS, and no third-party script touching the DOM.

  Two tiers, so the strip is never a hole in the homepage:
  1. The metafield, whenever it holds at least one usable entry. Each tile
     links to its own post.
  2. The seven bundled studio photos otherwise — which, with the Worker not
     deployed, is what actually renders today. Those tiles link to the profile
     instead, since there is no per-post URL to send anyone to.

  Entries with no `src` are skipped rather than drawn as a broken tile, and
  the live count is taken *after* that filter — so a metafield holding only
  malformed entries falls back rather than rendering an empty strip.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign feed = shop.metafields.ness.instagram_feed.value

  comment
    Seven, up from the five the original design drew. The cap lives here
    rather than in either loop so both the live feed and the bundled photos
    stay the same length and the "N of M" labels can never disagree.

    The strip already scrolls horizontally and each tile holds a fixed 4:5
    frame, so the count is free to change without touching the layout — the
    row simply runs longer. It is still a cap, not a target: a metafield
    holding fewer entries renders fewer tiles.
  endcomment
  assign max_tiles = 7

  assign live_count = 0
  for post in feed limit: max_tiles
    if post.src != blank
      assign live_count = live_count | plus: 1
    endif
  endfor

  comment
    The profile link has no schema default — Shopify's url setting type does
    not accept one — so the known profile stands in if the field is cleared.
    Same three-tier thinking as the header logo: a fresh install is never
    broken and nobody has to remember this step.
  endcomment
  assign profile_url = section.settings.profile_url | default: 'https://www.instagram.com/nessclusive'
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  id="luxury" is a fixed anchor, not a setting — the hero's quick links scroll
  here by that name, and letting it be edited would break them silently. It was
  "looks" until the revised design renamed it; the hero's dropdown value moves
  with it. scroll-margin-top clears the floating header.
{%- endcomment -%}
<section
  class="ness-instagram"
  id="luxury"
  {% if section.settings.heading != blank %}aria-labelledby="ness-instagram-heading-{{ section.id }}"{% endif %}
>
  {%- if section.settings.eyebrow != blank or section.settings.heading != blank or section.settings.follow_label != blank -%}
    <div class="ness-instagram__intro" data-reveal="0">
      {%- if section.settings.eyebrow != blank -%}
        <p class="ness-instagram__eyebrow">{{ section.settings.eyebrow | escape }}</p>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if section.settings.heading != blank -%}
        <h2 class="ness-instagram__heading" id="ness-instagram-heading-{{ section.id }}">
          {{- section.settings.heading | escape -}}
        </h2>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if section.settings.follow_label != blank -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          The follow link goes to the profile, never to a post — it is the one
          place in this section that always uses the profile URL. aria-label
          repeats the visible wording before adding the new-tab warning, so the
          accessible name still contains the label the merchant typed.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <a
          class="ness-instagram__follow"
          href="{{ profile_url | escape }}"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          aria-label="{{ section.settings.follow_label | escape }}, opens in a new tab"
        >
          {{- section.settings.follow_label | escape -}}
        </a>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Purely decorative rule under the heading block. It carries no meaning,
        so it is hidden from assistive tech rather than announced as an empty
        element.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <span class="ness-instagram__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Each tile is an image-only link, so the accessible name goes on the anchor
    and the image is marked decorative. The feed contract carries no caption or
    alt text, and a bundled photo has no alt field either, so there is nothing
    truthful to put in alt="" — an invented description would be worse than
    none. The anchor label also announces the new tab, which target="_blank"
    otherwise springs on screen reader users unannounced.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="ness-instagram__strip">
    {%- if live_count > 0 -%}
      {%- assign tile_number = 0 -%}
      {%- for post in feed limit: max_tiles -%}
        {%- if post.src != blank -%}
          {%- assign tile_number = tile_number | plus: 1 -%}
          {%- assign post_url = post.permalink | default: profile_url -%}
          <a
            class="ness-instagram__tile"
            href="{{ post_url | escape }}"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
            aria-label="Instagram post {{ tile_number }} of {{ live_count }}, opens in a new tab"
          >
            {%- comment -%}
              Rendered at the URL the Worker stored, untouched by image_url:
              the src may be a Shopify Files URL or — in the simpler Worker
              variant — a raw Instagram CDN one, and image_url handles only
              the first. Sizing is the Worker's job. The tile's fixed 4:5
              frame means neither choice can shift the layout.

              width/height describe the frame, not the file: the source's own
              dimensions are unknown here and irrelevant, because the tile
              crops to 4:5 either way. Stating the rendered ratio is what
              reserves the right space before the photo arrives.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              onerror swaps in a bundled tile if the stored URL ever 404s.

              It should not happen — the Worker mirrors images into Shopify
              Files precisely so the URLs are permanent. But if a file is
              deleted in admin, or the Worker fell back to a raw Instagram CDN
              link after a failed upload and that link later expired, the
              alternative is a broken-image icon sitting on the homepage until
              someone notices. The handler removes itself first so a missing
              fallback cannot loop.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- assign tile_fallback = 'ness-ig-fallback-'
              | append: tile_number
              | append: '.jpg'
              | asset_url
            -%}
            <img
              class="ness-instagram__image"
              src="{{ post.src | escape }}"
              alt=""
              width="640"
              height="800"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
              onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src='{{ tile_fallback }}';"
            >
          </a>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    {%- else -%}
      {%- for i in (1..max_tiles) -%}
        {%- assign fallback_image = 'ness-ig-fallback-' | append: i | append: '.jpg' -%}
        <a
          class="ness-instagram__tile"
          href="{{ profile_url | escape }}"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          aria-label="Nessclusive on Instagram, photo {{ i }} of {{ max_tiles }}, opens in a new tab"
        >
          <img
            class="ness-instagram__image"
            src="{{ fallback_image | asset_url }}"
            alt=""
            width="640"
            height="800"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          >
        </a>
      {%- endfor -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</section>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Instagram",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The photos are live — they come straight from the Instagram account and refresh on their own, so there is nothing to upload here. Only the wording and the profile link are editable; the number of tiles, their shape, and all colours and spacing are fixed by the design."
    },
    { "type": "text", "id": "eyebrow", "label": "Small heading above", "default": "On Instagram" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "Heading", "default": "Luxury Looks" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "follow_label", "label": "Follow link wording", "default": "Follow @Nessclusive", "info": "Opens the Instagram profile below. Leave empty to hide the link." },
    { "type": "url", "id": "profile_url", "label": "Instagram profile", "info": "Where the follow link goes. It is also where the tiles go while the live feed is unavailable and the five bundled photos are showing — live tiles always open their own post instead. Leave empty to use instagram.com/nessclusive." }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
